msgid "Quickly jump to..."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:19
msgid ""
"[A](#a) [B](#b) [C](#c) [D](#d) [E](#e) [F](#f) [G](#g) [H](#h) [I](#i) [J]"
"(#j) [K](#k) [L](#l) [M](#m)  [N](#n) [O](#o) [P](#p) [Q](#q) [R](#r) [S]"
"(#s) [T](#t) [U](#u) [V](#v) [W](#w) [X](#x) [Y](#y) [Z](#z)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:22
#, no-wrap
msgid "A"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:31
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Admin\n"
": An admin(istrator) is a user in Zammad who has special rights.\n"
"  Admins can configure user accesses, time recording settings, templates,\n"
"  and text modules and, on a higher level, integrations, reporting, etc.\n"
"  So if you're looking to make a change within your Zammad and you find that\n"
"  it doesn't work, find an admin in your organization and ask them - chances\n"
"  are, they can help.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:37
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Agent\n"
": An agent is what we call a user in Zammad who processes tickets/inquiries.\n"
"  There are usually several or many agents who use Zammad regularly and\n"
"  sometimes even consider it their main tool. Some of them are admins,\n"
"  meaning that they can change settings, user rights, and so on (see above).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:44
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"API\n"
": An API (Application Programming Interface) is a connection that allows you\n"
"  to create or modify business objects. Zammad has a REST\n"
"  ([Representational State Transfer](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Representational_state_transfer))\n"
"  API that allows our users, among other things, to connect third-party systems\n"
"  to their instance (such as social media or messengers).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:47
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  You can learn more on our\n"
"  [API landing page](https://zammad.com/en/product/features/rest-api).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:53
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Automation\n"
": There are many processes that can be automated with Zammad.\n"
"  This means that certain steps or actions take place automatically,\n"
"  hence no further action is required from the agents.\n"
"  One example would be the weekly deletion of tickets at a pre-defined time.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:58
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Article\n"
": Each correspondence within a ticket is called an article. Ticket articles\n"
"  can be internal (so only agents can see them) or public\n"
"  (e.g. emails to your customers, which they receive, too).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:63
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Autosave\n"
": Autosave might be one of the coolest traits of Zammad:\n"
"  It saves all your work as you go along, so if you ever log out unexpectedly\n"
"  or your browser crashes (we've all been there…) none of your work gets lost!\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:66
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  You can learn more on our\n"
"  [Autosave landing page](https://zammad.com/en/product/features/autosave).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:67
#, no-wrap
msgid "B"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:73
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Branding\n"
": Every company has a different identity, and Zammad accommodates this in\n"
"  various ways, e.g. by letting you design your signatures freely and add\n"
"  your company logo to the platform.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:74
#, no-wrap
msgid "C"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:84
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Custom Development (CD)\n"
": We are constantly working on improving Zammad, and we keep adding new\n"
"  features with every single release. However, sometimes our customers might\n"
"  require a very specific new feature, addition, or adjustment that is either\n"
"  very urgent or very particular to their individual use case.\n"
"  This is when a custom development can take place: We offer the customer to\n"
"  develop the desired feature at a price that we agree upon previously\n"
"  (which is based on the expected hours needed for completion).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:88
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Changelog\n"
": With every new release comes a new changelog. It is basically a list of all\n"
"  the things that have changed, from new enhancements to bug fixes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:90
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
#| msgid "Supported with bug and security fixes - see also our [Security Policy](https://github.com/zammad/zammad/blob/develop/SECURITY.md)."
msgid "  You can find them all on our [GitHub](https://github.com/zammad/zammad/blob/stable/CHANGELOG.md)!\n"
msgstr "Подржано је са исправкама грешака и сигурносних пропуста – погледајте и нашу [безбедносну полису](https://github.com/zammad/zammad/blob/develop/SECURITY.md)."

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:100
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Customer\n"
": A customer is a person that you communicate with from within Zammad.\n"
"  Every customer receives a profile page, which we call the\n"
"  Customer Information Page,\n"
"  and it shows all the tickets of this particular customer.\n"
"  Various customers can be assigned to the same organization.\n"
"  Each customer can access their individual\n"
"  Customer Interface, where they see all their tickets with the current\n"
"  status and live updates.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:104
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Content Sidebar\n"
": Right sidebar in the ticket view which includes the sidebar tabs and the\n"
"  active sidebar (if opened).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:111
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Core Workflows\n"
": This feature allows every organization to configure their individual\n"
"  dynamic fields and ticket masks based on their specific workflows.\n"
"  This way, certain Groups will only (or always) see certain fields.\n"
"  You can even set up dependencies, as in, if one field is filled in,\n"
"  another one opens up or becomes mandatory.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:117
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"CTI\n"
": CTI stands for Computer Telephony Integration and allows you to collect\n"
"  detailed information on all your incoming and outgoing calls.\n"
"  This includes, for example, a call log, an overview of which agent is\n"
"  currently on a call, a caller ID search, and even a Do-Not-Disturb-Mode.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:124
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Checkmk\n"
": Checkmk is a powerful IT monitoring tool that can send real-time status\n"
"  alerts to Zammad via email or REST API. Set these alerts up in Checkmk,\n"
"  and Zammad will automatically create, update, and close tickets based on\n"
"  the health of your system.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:131
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Clearbit\n"
": Clearbit is a marketing data engine designed to collect information on your\n"
"  contacts. Thus, new queries from unknown users in Zammad can be automatically\n"
"  enriched with information such as company, number of employees,\n"
"  annual turnover, industry, and much more.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:138
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Conflict Warning\n"
": When two agents edit the same ticket at the same time, a lot can go wrong -\n"
"  from duplicate replies to overwritten messages. Zammad helps you to avoid\n"
"  this with its integrated conflict warning. So if you see another agents\n"
"  avatar and a little pen at the bottom of your ticket, it means they are\n"
"  currently editing it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:139
#, no-wrap
msgid "D"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:148
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Dashboard\n"
": The dashboard is every agent's individual Welcome Page in Zammad and gives\n"
"  you feedback on your situation by collecting information and statistics\n"
"  about your work.\n"
"  Here you can find all sorts of overviews, such as the open tickets,\n"
"  the average waiting time, or the reopening rate. You can also see what your\n"
"  colleagues are doing by checking the Activity Stream.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:153
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Dialog\n"
": A dialog is a element which pops up in the middle of the screen because of its\n"
"  importance. An example could be the dialog which asks you to save or discard\n"
"  your unsaved changes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:159
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Documentation\n"
": If this Glossary isn't enough already (just kidding, it won't be),\n"
"  our Documentation is the place to head to for all information on Zammad,\n"
"  especially when it comes to the more technical aspects,\n"
"  such as settings or installations.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:160
#, no-wrap
msgid "E"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:168
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Escalation\n"
": An escalation is what happens after the deadline for a ticket has passed and,\n"
"  for example, no update to the customer has been created. The ticket is marked\n"
"  in red in your taskbar and the overviews and everyone else who is involved in\n"
"  its process gets very sad. So don't let tickets escalate! Also, in order to\n"
"  prevent escalations, you can use our SLAs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:175
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"External Authentication\n"
": External authentication is an easy, one-click option for your users to log\n"
"  into Zammad. It has various benefits: not only is it faster but it also\n"
"  means that your users will have to remember fewer passwords.\n"
"  Zammad currently supports more than ten login providers, such as Facebook,\n"
"  GitHub, GitLab, Google, or Microsoft / Office365.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:181
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Exchange Integration\n"
": The Exchange integration allows users to sync their contacts from their\n"
"  Exchange address book with Zammad. This way, every time a contact is updated\n"
"  in Exchange, the iteration will be reflected in Zammad, giving you direct\n"
"  access to all your Exchange contacts from within your helpdesk.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:185
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Elasticsearch\n"
": Zammad offers an Elasticsearch integration (a free and open search engine)\n"
"  that makes the search process within Zammad super fast and powerful.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced div block (info <Badge type="info" text="Cloud Service only" />)
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:186
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\n"
"  Via a read-only user in Elasticsearch, you can also integrate your\n"
"  favorite reporting tool (e.g. like Grafana).\n"
"\n"
"  While technically self hosted users can enable such a behavior\n"
"  as well it is out of our documentation scope.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:195
#, no-wrap
msgid "F"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:201
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Feature\n"
": A feature is what we call the different functionalities of Zammad,\n"
"  such as our integrations, productivity tools, or time-saving aspects.\n"
"  We keep adding new features with every release.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:208
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Feature Request\n"
": Users can let us know if they are missing a particular feature in Zammad.\n"
"  We collect all of their wishes\n"
"  in our Community in the Feature Request category.\n"
"  If a request comes in regularly and we feel that it would be a\n"
"  great addition, we'll put it on our roadmap and start working on it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:214
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Feature Sponsoring\n"
": If an organization urgently requires one of the features on the list,\n"
"  they can fast-forward the development and put it on top of the list by\n"
"  sponsoring it, which means that they cover the costs for the development.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:215
#, no-wrap
msgid "G"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:224
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Groups\n"
": Groups are a synonym for departments or processing groups.\n"
"  The incoming tickets are assigned to them.\n"
"  The corresponding group is responsible for the processing.\n"
"  Within the group, an owner can be defined, who is then responsible for\n"
"  this ticket. Access rights to tickets are also controlled via the groups.\n"
"  The possible permissions are \"full access\", \"read-only\" and \"no access\".\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:228
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  If you have worked with the OTRS system in the past,\n"
"  you might remember the principle of \"queues\".\n"
"  The groups in Zammad are the same as the queues in OTRS.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:233
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Grafana\n"
": Grafana is an open-source reporting tool.\n"
"  Zammad users on the Plus plan can integrate it into their instance and\n"
"  receive detailed analytics on their performance.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:236
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Hooking up Grafana to Elasticsearch is an possibility you can do on your\n"
"  on-premise installation as well.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:241
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"GitHub\n"
": GitHub is a service for the version management of software development\n"
"  projects. It uses Git, a software that tracks changes in file sets.\n"
"  Here at Zammad, we use it to maintain our repository.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:244
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  As Zammad in an open-source project, many developers and tech-lovers from all\n"
"  over the world contribute to it. GitHub is where we coordinate all of this.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:246
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
#| msgid "The Zammad source code can be found on GitHub in the [Zammad repository](https://github.com/zammad/zammad)."
msgid "  You can find the repository [here](https://github.com/zammad/zammad).\n"
msgstr "Изворни код Zammad-а можете пронаћи на GitHub-у у [Zammad репозиторију](https://github.com/zammad/zammad)."

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:250
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Besides our own repo, Zammad also has an integration for GitHub.\n"
"  It creates a data exchange that shows you all relevant information about your\n"
"  issues directly in the helpdesk, such as status or assignees.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:255
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"GitLab\n"
": GitLab is similar to GitHub.\n"
"  Here at Zammad, we use it for our internal development.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:259
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  There is also an integration that allows users to connect GitLab to Zammad\n"
"  so that all their issues and their corresponding changes are reflected in\n"
"  both systems.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:260
#, no-wrap
msgid "H"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:263
#, no-wrap
msgid "I"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:272
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"i-doit\n"
": i-doit is a CMDB (Configuration Management Data Base).\n"
"  It helps you to keep an eye on every piece of the physical and digital\n"
"  infrastructure. A corresponding integration makes it possible to connect\n"
"  it to Zammad, where it adds a new tab to Zammad's ticket sidebar so\n"
"  you can link to existing i-doit objects for easy reference.\n"
"  It also allows you to create Zammad tickets in i-doit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:278
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Icinga\n"
": Icinga is a monitoring system that supervises the availability of an\n"
"  organization's entire system infrastructure.\n"
"  It can be integrated into Zammad so that it triggers a ticket in case\n"
"  of a warning situation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:282
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Issue-Tracking System\n"
": Issue trackers are usually systems that track processes on a technical level.\n"
"  Two of the best-known examples are GitHub and GitLab.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:286
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Zammad is also often referred to as an issue-tracking system.\n"
"  However, as a helpdesk, it focuses on communication at the customer\n"
"  level rather than the technical level.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:287
#, no-wrap
msgid "J"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:290
#, no-wrap
msgid "K"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:296
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Knowledge Base\n"
": Think of a very extensive set of FAQs - that's exactly what the\n"
"  Zammad knowledge base is. It collects all important information:\n"
"  definitions, processes, how-to's, organigrams, etc.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:301
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Knowledge base articles can be either internal or external, so you can\n"
"  either show them to the world (good for information on your product or\n"
"  service, for example) or keep them for your team\n"
"  (e.g. for internal processes or team info).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:306
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Kibana <Badge type=\"info\" text=\"on-premise only\" />\n"
": Kibana is a browser-based, open-source reporting tool that focuses on data\n"
"  evaluation. It was developed by Elastic, which is why it is not a surprise\n"
"  that it uses data from Elasticsearch for its analytics.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:309
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Kibana can be integrated with Zammad, allowing for helpdesk data to be\n"
"  mapped in the reporting tool.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:310
#, no-wrap
msgid "L"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:316
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"LDAP\n"
": A Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) helps provide information\n"
"  about your users within Zammad. Authentication of users against the LDAP\n"
"  and LDAP role mapping to Zammad roles are also possible.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:317
#, no-wrap
msgid "M"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:324
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Macro\n"
": A macro is a series of actions. By starting the macro, the actions are also\n"
"  triggered (like a domino effect), so users don't have to work through each\n"
"  individual step separately.\n"
"  It saves an enormous amount of time and ensures that no step is forgotten.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:330
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  An example of this is declaring a ticket as \"spam\".\n"
"  The manual way here would be to assign an owner, set a status, and add the\n"
"  tag \"spam\". Using a macro, all this can be done in just one action.\n"
"  Macros can be used in the ticket zoom or within an overview\n"
"  (using multiple selection).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:336
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Migrator / Migration Wizard\n"
": If a company wants to switch from another helpdesk software to Zammad,\n"
"  they often have one concern: What about their existing data?\n"
"  That's why we have built our migration wizards that help with migrating all\n"
"  data at the touch of a button.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:341
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Monit\n"
": Monit is an open-source monitoring tool that relies on a simple setup and\n"
"  a strong community. You can integrate it with Zammad - this way,\n"
"  a ticket is created every time you receive an email in Monit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:346
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Mentions\n"
": Mentions are a Zammad feature that allows you to tag another agent in a\n"
"  ticket. Just type ``@@`` and the name. The selected person will be notified\n"
"  and will be watching the ticket from now on.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:347
#, no-wrap
msgid "N"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:352
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Navigation\n"
": Navigation means the left sidebar in Zammad which contains navigation tabs,\n"
"  the search, notifications and many more items, depending on your system.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:356
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Navigation Tab\n"
": One element of the navigation. You can have multiple tabs open and quickly\n"
"  switch between them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:362
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Nagios\n"
": Nagios is another monitoring tool that alerts IT teams when, for example,\n"
"  a server is no longer accessible or a hard disk is about to be exhausted.\n"
"  Nagios can be integrated with Zammad so that a ticket is created in case of\n"
"  an alert.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:363
#, no-wrap
msgid "O"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:369
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"On-Premise\n"
": This term means that Zammad is not provided as a cloud service of the\n"
"  company Zammad, but runs on a server which is controlled by a user or\n"
"  customer of Zammad.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:376
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Owner\n"
": The owner of a ticket is the person responsible for it and ensures that it\n"
"  is processed in the best possible way.\n"
"  Of course, ownership can be transferred to another agent.\n"
"  In this case, it is recommended to leave a handover note on the ticket so\n"
"  that the new owner knows what is expected of them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:382
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Organization\n"
": An organization identifies a grouping of customers that operate under the\n"
"  same roof or within the same customer group.\n"
"  If a customer whose organization is \"sharing\" logs in to the customer\n"
"  interface, this user has access to all tickets of his organization.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:383
#, no-wrap
msgid "P"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:390
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Parent/Child Relationship\n"
": If one ticket results in other subtasks (or additional correspondences),\n"
"  you can split it into several tickets. The main one will then be the parent\n"
"  ticket and the tickets with related subtopics are children.\n"
"  By the way: in the same way, you can also merge two tickets into one.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:396
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Placetel\n"
": Placetel is a Cloud Telephone System that allows users to make phone calls\n"
"  via VoIP. Use your regular phone number and call someone directly on their\n"
"  mobile or landline while still having all communication in one place\n"
"  (aka Zammad).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:401
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Integrating a Placetel account with Zammad provides users with a call log,\n"
"  making the history of their correspondences even more accurate.\n"
"  Callers are identified directly by their caller ID - a key function that\n"
"  saves agents a lot of time when assigning callers.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:406
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Priority\n"
": Every ticket gets assigned a priority.\n"
"  By default, the priority is 2 (normal).\n"
"  But it can be changed to either 1 (low) or 3 (high).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:407
#, no-wrap
msgid "Q"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:410
#, no-wrap
msgid "R"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:415
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Role\n"
": Everyone who logs into Zammad has a predefined role. There are three types:\n"
"  admin, agent, and customer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:418
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Admins have the most rights: they can define roles, permissions,\n"
"  and settings for the entire team and instance.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:421
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Agents can view and edit tickets, but not change any settings other than\n"
"  those of their own profiles.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:424
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Customers can view their tickets' processing status in their\n"
"  individual Customer Interface.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:428
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Release\n"
": Every few months, we bring a new version of Zammad into the world,\n"
"  which is called a release. It all started with Zammad 1.0.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:434
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Every release adds new features to our software.\n"
"  There are major and minor releases:\n"
"  major releases (such as Zammad 1.0, 2.0, etc.) bring major changes.\n"
"  Minor releases are installed on top of them (such as 1.1, 2.1, etc.)\n"
"  and bring smaller updates.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:438
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  In our [release notes](https://zammad.com/en/releases) you can find what is\n"
"  included in new versions and also important Information about what you should\n"
"  consider before updating Zammad.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:439
#, no-wrap
msgid "S"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:444
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Second Level Navigation\n"
": Refers to an additional menu next to the navigation (e.g. when opening profile\n"
"  settings).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:449
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"SSO\n"
": Single-sign-on (SSO) allows you to access all your systems and devices with\n"
"  just one login. There are various providers that make this process easy and\n"
"  secure. Zammad currently supports SSO via SAML and Shibboleth.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:454
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"S/MIME\n"
": S/MIME is the most widely-supported method for secure email communication.\n"
"  By activating it in Zammad, all messages sent from Zammad will be signed and\n"
"  encrypted.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:460
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Sipgate\n"
": Sipgate is a SaaS solution for internet telephony.\n"
"  The Zammad integration for Sipgate provides users with a detailed call\n"
"  overview. If you have a customer that wants to get connected to a certain\n"
"  agent, the caller log will tell you if this colleague is currently available.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:464
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Side(Panel)\n"
": This is the panel which is flying in on the right side for actions like ticket\n"
"  linking or ticket merging.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:469
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Sidebar Tab\n"
": In the content sidebar, there are different tabs like \"Customer\",\n"
"  \"Organization\" and \"Ticket\", depending on your system and ticket. These are\n"
"  called sidebar tabs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:471
#, no-wrap
msgid "  An active sidebar tab is the currently opened sidebar tab.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:478
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"SLA\n"
": A Service Level Agreement (SLA) is a contract between an end-user and a\n"
"  company that defines the minimum expected service requirements including\n"
"  quality, availability, and punctuality.\n"
"  They are used to set expectations and hold companies accountable for keeping\n"
"  their promises.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:482
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  You can easily set up SLAs in Zammad and define parameters such as the time\n"
"  for the first response, an update, and a solution. Once the deadline has been\n"
"  reached, the ticket will escalate.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:486
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"State\n"
": Every ticket has a state. You can change it once you've updated the ticket.\n"
"  There are four types of states, and they are all color-coded.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:487
#, no-wrap
msgid "T"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:494
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Text Module\n"
": If you find that you send the same answers / text bits over and over again,\n"
"  you can save yourself a bunch of work and create a text module.\n"
"  This way, you just need to type ``::`` shortcut and the pre-defined paragraph\n"
"  will automatically appear in your article.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:497
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  For example, here at Zammad, we have a text module with the shortcut\n"
"  ``::ilff``, which turns into ``I look forward to your feedback``.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:502
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(Ticket) Template\n"
": If you create many similar tickets or write many similar texts, you can\n"
"  create a template for them. This is helpful for introductions to your\n"
"  product/service or for drawing up an offer. It's a real time-saver!\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:510
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Tags\n"
": Tags help you to categorize tickets. You can define them based on your use\n"
"  case. For example, if you're a retail business, your tags could be based on\n"
"  your product categories to help you organize tickets by the type of product\n"
"  they refer to.\n"
"  But they could also be based on the type of request, e.g.\n"
"  refund, delivery issue, missing…\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:511
#, no-wrap
msgid "U"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:518
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"User\n"
": A user is any user of the ticket system. Each user is assigned certain\n"
"  permissions, which allow them to access certain areas and information.\n"
"  Users can have various roles, with the standard options being agent, admin,\n"
"  and customer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:519
#, no-wrap
msgid "V"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:522
#, no-wrap
msgid "W"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:529
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Webhooks\n"
": In a nutshell, webhooks are an easy way for systems to communicate with each\n"
"  other and allow you to send real-time data to any other application.\n"
"  We use them to allow our users to inform a third-party system about new\n"
"  information in Zammad.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:530
#, no-wrap
msgid "X"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:533
#, no-wrap
msgid "Y"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:536
#, no-wrap
msgid "Z"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:540
msgid "Zammad : Zammad is the greatest helpdesk in the world. Period."
msgstr ""

